"Plots paired method differences against their means to assess bias and the spread of agreement.\n\nUse when: Two methods measure the same quantity on the same observational units. Agreement, rather than correlation alone, determines interchangeability.\n\nAvoid when: Repeated measurements or magnitude-dependent variability are ignored by a simple independent-difference analysis.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes the mean of each paired measurement. Vertical position encodes their signed difference, with bias and defined agreement limits as reference lines.\n\nBuild: Match paired measurements and declare the subtraction direction. Calculate mean difference and limits using an appropriate distributional model. Plot points, bias, and labeled agreement limits, with uncertainty for those estimates when available.\n\nCheck: Check units, pairing, and difference direction are consistent. Inspect magnitude-dependent spread and nonnormal differences. Distinguish limits of agreement from confidence intervals around mean bias.\n\nPitfalls: High correlation does not imply close agreement. Approximate mean ±1.96 SD limits require justified assumptions and are not automatically acceptable domain tolerances.\n\nExample: Assess synthetic measurements from two scales by plotting scale-A minus scale-B against their mean and labeling estimated agreement limits.\n\nAlternatives: Youden plot, Scatterplot, Residual plot."
